{"kind":"promise","text":"I will independently review the completed chess identity lifecycle at exact head c40dbf6958614a811ecd81790f695696ddf47205.\n\nChecked before promising, because a verdict against a stale or wrong-commit artifact cannot merge: all four artifacts are at that exact head, live, fresh, unretired and not world-stale — 887e9294 chess.go, fce2a9ef chess_test.go, a8eeb379 cmd/chess, 34430517 README.md. The head is one commit ahead of main at 94f5c73 and zero behind, so there is no carry to audit and no recut in prospect. 494 insertions across five files.\n\nWHAT I WILL BE HARDEST ON, because it is the thing the request is actually about. Hugh's finding was that the identity mutation surface can be driven but not observed. The failure mode that follows is a specific one: reporting the success of an append as though it were the success of an authority change. Those are different facts. An endorsement can be written to the log and still confer nothing, because the signer had no standing, because a later record already superseded it, or because the readback cannot be resolved at all. So I will check that created, refused and unknown each come from verified post-append resolution rather than from the append's own error being nil; that the appended record must be the effective standing record before anything is called created; and that a failed readback is reported as unknown rather than optimistically as success. Fail-closed on unknown is the property that matters here, and I will look for the place where it is not.\n\nRevocation gets the same treatment, and the counterfactual is the part I want to understand rather than accept. Distinguishing an actual revocation from a no-op needs more than observing that the record landed, and codex says an opaque same-position, same-timestamp counterfactual does that work. I will check that it genuinely separates force from filing, and I will look specifically at wrong signer, inherited credential withdrawal, and expiry edges, since those are where a revocation can appear to have worked while changing nothing.\n\nFor ListAnchors: that verified AnchorSchema is used only to find candidates and never to answer authority; that current authority is resolved before anything is returned; exact subject and scope filtering rather than prefix; deduplication; the 1..100 cap; and truncation and head reported without leaking custody.\n\nFor the MCP surface: shipped anchor, list_anchors and revoke_anchor schemas, strict argument handling, tools/list coverage, and that existing generic chess actionResult behaviour is unchanged rather than quietly reshaped.\n\nI will reproduce the mutation myself rather than reading it: making anchor success unconditional must fail TestIdentityMutationOutcomeDistinguishesEffectiveAndRefusedAnchors on the standing-less endorsement, and the tree must be byte-clean afterwards. I will also try at least one mutation codex did not name, because a mutation the author chose is evidence the test bites where they looked, not everywhere.\n\nGates from a clean detached exact-head checkout: gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check, the focused lifecycle and tool tests, and go test ./... -count=1. I will record architecture, security and simplification conclusions and file one verdict with gs review naming the exact head and all four artifacts. I will not edit and will not merge.\n\nSequencing, so nobody is guessing: hugh named the cloned-repository how-to his highest priority and its review is with codex at 03fd25e5; nothing in this promise competes with that. My open query-sandbox verdict ad272998 is filed and needs no more from me."}